### Medium impact, medium-high effort — architecture-dependent

**D3 — Lean verification of reconciliation properties** — DONE (2026-04-24)
`research/fm/lean/CertifiedAtc/ObservationReconciliation.lean` added (~200 lines).
Proves 9 theorems across 4 reconciliation functions (departure, arrival, ground × 2):
terminality (4), monotonicity (3), `dep_airborne_advances`, `arr_runway_advances`.
All proofs closed by `cases s <;> cases p <;> decide` or `<;> simp_all [...]`.
No Kotlin changes; sits alongside existing `CertifiedAtc` Lean work in `research/fm/lean/`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**A9 — Manual routes in `LowgSpike` and `ArrivalVerticalTest`**
Tests bypass the planner, creating a conformance gap. Migrate to planner-driven routes once
IFR wiring lands.
Impact: M | Effort: M (blocked on IFR wiring)

**B7 — APP→TWR handoff at downwind (phraseologically wrong)**
Documented in code. Correct handoff is ~8NM final or ILS intercept for IFR. Blocked on
approach sequencing work — fixing in isolation doesn't make sense.
Impact: M | Effort: H (blocked on APP sequencing)

**F1 — Holding pattern semantics provisional**
`HoldingPattern` is structurally minimal — no racetrack geometry, no entry procedure, no
timing. Needed for realistic IFR holds. Blocked on geometry work.
Impact: M | Effort: H

**RR-1 — ICAO 4444 broad baseline still too wide for blind downstream use**
The full-document normalizer now passes machine gate and the downstream baseline policy blocks
`manual_review_required` bundles plus `label_stub`/`table_fragment` structural debt, but the
mechanically eligible baseline still contains hundreds of bundles. Only the seeded trio
(`4.3.2.1.2`, `4.3.2.1.3`, `4.5.7.5.1`) should currently drive downstream consumers. We still
need staged rollout rules and more golden coverage before widening beyond that set.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-3 — Ollama-first transfer-family structure pass compresses nested enumerations** — DONE (2026-04-24)
The new Ollama-first requirement pipeline works on raw `ICAO 4444` slices, but the transfer-family
run over `4.3.2.1.1`-`4.3.2.1.3` still showed structural compression in the first pass: the model
flattened part of the arriving-aircraft substructure, effectively losing the separate `b)` branch
and blending nested list material into the `a)` item. Multi-attempt extraction plus reconciliation
helped candidate stability, but this structure defect still pushed later adjudication toward
`needs_bundle` / `needs_split`. Repeated structure attempts plus structure reconciliation now
preserve the nested transfer hierarchy in the transfer-family run. Remaining issues are tracked
under `RR-4`.
Impact: M | Effort: M

**RR-4 — Ollama challenger sometimes ignores source modality when disputing authority**
In the structure-reconciled `ICAO 4444` transfer-family run, the challenger disputed a `should`
clause and a source Note as if they should be authoritative requirements. The judge corrected both
to `advisory_only`, but this is still noisy and could waste review attention. Add a modality/authority
check to the Ollama-first validation workflow, or tighten the challenger prompt so `wrong_authority`
claims must explicitly address the source modality (`shall`, `should`, `may`, `Note`).
Impact: M | Effort: M

